imx95-navqb: disable the BT UART that stalls boot for 60s

The board dts enables an 88W8987 Bluetooth controller on lpuart4, but the chip
never answers btnxpuart's bootloader handshake:

    Bluetooth: hci0: FW Download Timeout. offset: 0

"offset: 0" means not a single bootloader signature byte was received. The wait in
nxp_download_firmware() is a hardcoded 60000 ms and btnxpuart has no module
parameters, so every boot pays a fixed 60s penalty before failing. That also stalls
udev long enough for NetworkManager-wait-online to give up.

The likely cause is firmware ownership: NXP's out-of-tree Wi-Fi driver (moal) brings
up the combo part over SDIO (sduart8987_combo.bin) before btnxpuart probes, leaving
the chip past its bootloader, so the UART side never re-enters download mode. The
module reset is also not asserted as described -- the kernel rejects this board's
reset-gpios flags:

    reset-gpio code does not support GPIO flags 7 for GPIO 16

(usdhc3_pwrseq, i2c3_ioexp23 pin 16, GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN = 7)

Bluetooth is unused on this integration and there is no BT userspace in the image,
so disable the node rather than pay the timeout. Re-enable it together with either
a Wi-Fi-only moal firmware (sd8987_wlan.bin, leaving uart8987_bt.bin to btnxpuart)
or a userspace btattach flow on the already-running combo firmware.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [NavQ95 board integration; should be fixed in NXP-Robotics/linux-imx]
Signed-off-by: Leonardo Bornia <leonardo.bornia@toradex.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/freescale/imx95-navqb.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/freescale/imx95-navqb.dts
@@ -884,7 +884,7 @@
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&pinctrl_uart4>;
 	uart-has-rtscts;
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 
 	bluetooth {
 		compatible = "nxp,88w8987-bt";
 